---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import PaletteHue from "../../components/Blog/PaletteHue.astro"
import Severities from "../../component-examples/callout/Severities.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="callout-relative-colors">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A callout with severities needs a background, border, text and icon color
      for each one, in light and in dark. Five severities times four colors
      times two schemes is 40 hand-picked values, plus a media query or theme
      class to switch between them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Add a severity or tweak a hue and you pick them all again. Preprocessors
      could compute shades, but only at build time, not from a custom property.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Each severity class sets a single <code>--ui-palette-source</code> color.
        It takes its hue from the severity role, so a theme that changes <code
          >--ui-info</code
        > recolors every info shade
      </li>
      <li>
        The palette derives 16 shades from it with <code>oklch(from …)</code>: a
        fixed lightness per step, chroma scaled from the source, the hue nudged
        {" "}<code>--ui-palette-hue-rotate-by</code> degrees per step
      </li>
      <li>
        The theme picks the shades for each role with <code>light-dark()</code>,
        so dark mode needs no media query. The callout only reads the roles
      </li>
      <li>
        The tint sits on a translucent <code>::before</code> layer over the opaque
        surface
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.info {
  --ui-palette-source: oklch(from var(--ui-info) 0.58 0.21 h);
  --ui-palette-hue-rotate-by: 1;
  --ui-color-3: oklch(from var(--ui-palette-source) 93% calc(0.1 * (c / 0.21)) calc(h + (2 * var(--ui-palette-hue-rotate-by))));
  --ui-color-6: oklch(from var(--ui-palette-source) 71% calc(0.19 * (c / 0.21)) calc(h + (5 * var(--ui-palette-hue-rotate-by))));
  --ui-palette-tint-subtle: light-dark(
    oklch(from var(--ui-color-3) l c h / 60%),
    oklch(from var(--ui-color-6) l c h / 5%)
  );
}

.callout.info {
  --bg-color: var(--ui-palette-tint-subtle);
  --border-color: var(--ui-palette-border);
  --icon-color: var(--ui-palette-accent);
  color: var(--ui-palette-strong);
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Drag the hue. It changes one custom property, <code>--ui-info</code>, and
      the 16 shades below follow, with every border, icon, text and tint in the
      callouts.
    </p>
    <Example showCode={false}>
      <PaletteHue />
    </Example>
    <p>
      Every severity is one source color. Switch the color scheme and the shades
      follow.
    </p>
    <Example column showCode={false}>
      <Severities />
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        There's no <code>@supports</code> fallback. The whole palette, grays included,
        is built from relative colors
      </li>
      <li>
        Without relative colors or <code>light-dark()</code>, the declarations
        that use them are invalid at computed-value time and fall back to their
        initial or inherited values, so the theme loses its colors, not just the
        callout
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
